CHAPTER V

RIGHTS OF VICTIMS

Article 9

Every woman and child victim of violence has the right to:

a. receive protection from family, police, the Public Prosecutor's Office, court, advocates, social institutions, community, Regional Government and/or other parties either temporarily or based on a protection order from the court;
b. health and psychological services according to medical needs;
c. special handling related to victim confidentiality;
d. accompaniment by social workers and legal assistance at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
e. spiritual guidance services;
f. the right to information on case developments;
g. the right to safe house (shelter) services;
h. the right to restitution;
i. the right to psychosocial services/recovery; and
j. the right to social reintegration.

Article 13

1. Forms of protection services for women and children victims of violence implemented by PPT include:
a. medical services, in the form of treatment and recovery of physical injuries aimed at restoring the physical condition of victims carried out by medical personnel and paramedics;
b. medico-legal services constitute a form of medical services for evidentiary purposes in the legal field;

12 -

c. psycho-social services constitute services provided by companions in the context of traumatic recovery of the victim's condition, including the provision of space and the victim's house to protect victims from threats and intimidation;
d. legal services to assist victims in undergoing the judicial process;
e. economic independence services in the form of services for skills training and providing economic access so that victims can be independent.
2. Further provisions regarding service mechanisms as referred to in paragraph (1) are implemented according to Standard Operating Procedures regulated by Mayor Regulation.

