a. individuals;
b. social community institutions;
c. non-governmental organizations;
d. educational institutions;
e. religious institutions;
f. private sector; and/or
g. mass media.

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES AND OBJECTIVES

Article 2

The implementation of protection for women and child victims of violence is carried out based on the principles of:

a. respect for human rights;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. victim protection;
e. best interests of victims; and
f. empowerment.

Article 19

The implementation steps for empowerment of women victims of violence as referred to in Article 18 shall be carried out by:
a. procuring the necessary requirements for entrepreneurship training for women victims of violence, in order to improve knowledge, attitude and business skills;
b. facilitating the implementation of various job training and skills training;
c. providing assistance in developing productive economic enterprises;
d. exploring cooperation with small, medium and large enterprises as well as financial institutions to develop businesses of women victims of violence;
e. endeavoring to provide capital for women victims of violence; and
f. expanding access to information and promoting the results of productive economic enterprises of women victims of violence.

Article 3

1. The implementation of protection for women and child victims of violence aims to:
a. prevent all forms of violence;
b. protect victims of violence;

7 -

c. take action against perpetrators of violence; and
d. maintain the integrity of a harmonious and prosperous household.
2. Violence as referred to in paragraph (1) may take the form of:
a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. economic neglect; and
e. restriction of movement.

Article 3

paragraph (1) Sufficiently clear

paragraph (2)

letter a What is meant by "Physical violence" is an act that results in pain, illness or serious injury such as slapping, hitting, spitting, pulling hair, kicking, burning with hot objects, hitting/injuring with weapons and or causing disability to a person's body, miscarriage, unconsciousness and or causing death.

letter b What is meant by "Psychological Violence" is an act that results in fear, loss of self-confidence, loss of ability to act, feelings of helplessness, and/or severe psychological suffering to a person.

4 - letter c What is meant by "Sexual Violence" includes but is not limited to:

a. forced sexual intercourse committed against a person residing within the scope of that household and/or forced sexual intercourse against someone within the scope of their household with another person, for commercial purposes and/or certain purposes;

b. by force or threat of force compelling a woman who is not his wife to have sexual intercourse with him;

c. by force or threat of force compelling someone to commit or allow obscene acts to be committed;

d. intentionally using force or threat of force to compel a child to engage in sexual intercourse;

e. intentionally using force or threat of force, compelling, using deception, a series of lies or persuading a child to commit or allow obscene acts to be committed.

letter d What is meant by "economic neglect" is neglecting the economic needs of a wife or child or family member or relative such as not providing for the wife, even spending the wife's money, not providing or withholding the wages of domestic workers or drivers and including acts of restricting and/or prohibiting family members from working in decent employment.

letter e What is meant by "restriction of movement" is restricting the movement of a wife or child in developing their potential both for economic and social purposes such as prohibiting them from gathering and socializing in the community and restricting the movement of relatives such as preventing domestic workers or drivers from communicating or having contact with their families.

Article 16

letter a Handling of reports/complaints of women and children victims of violence, with the main indicator being the coverage of women and children victims of violence who receive complaint handling by trained officers in integrated service units.

Handling of complaints/reports of women and children victims of violence can also be measured through supporting indicators, namely the coverage of availability of officers in integrated service units who have the ability to follow up on community complaints about violence against women and children, in this case the ability to reach victims and follow up on complaints related to alleged violence against women and children;

6 - letter b Health services for women and children victims of violence, with the main indicator being the coverage of women and children victims of violence who receive health services by trained health workers at community health centers capable of managing KtP/A and PPT/PKT at Hospitals.

The health services referred to are services that include promotive, preventive, curative and rehabilitative aspects carried out by trained health workers, namely specialist doctors, doctors, dentists, nurses/midwives who have received training on medical management of violence against women and children victims of violence. The health services referred to are carried out in hospitals that provide integrated services and also for women and children at community health centers that provide comprehensive basic services.

Health services for women and children victims of violence can be measured through supporting indicators:  a. coverage of community health centers capable of managing KtP;
b. coverage of regional general hospitals/vertical general hospitals/regional general hospitals/private hospitals/Police hospitals that implement integrated services for women and children victims of violence;
c. coverage of trained health workers on case management of women and children victims of violence at community health centers; dand. coverage of health workers trained on case management of women and children victims of violence at hospitals.

letter c Social rehabilitation for women and children victims of violence with the main indicator being the coverage of social rehabilitation services provided by trained social rehabilitation officers for women and children victims of violence in integrated service units and the coverage of spiritual guidance services provided by trained spiritual guidance officers for women and children victims of violence in integrated service units.

7 - Social rehabilitation services for women and children victims of violence can also be measured through supporting indicators, namely the coverage of trained social rehabilitation officers and the coverage of trained officers in providing spiritual guidance.

letter d Law enforcement and legal aid for women and children victims of violence with the main indicators being the coverage of law enforcement from the investigation level to court decisions on cases of violence against women and children and the coverage of women and children victims of violence who receive legal aid services.

Law enforcement is the action of officials authorized by the State to carry out duties and functions as enforcers of applicable laws and regulations. Legal aid is law—everything related to aspects related to the legal field—provided to a person in the criminal or civil justice process.

Law enforcement for women and children victims of violence can also be measured through supporting indicators: a. coverage of completion of handling cases of violence against women and children at the police level;
b. coverage of availability of women and children service units (UPPA) at regional police and district/city police;
c. coverage of availability of facilities and infrastructure at UPPA;
d. coverage of availability of trained police in providing gender-sensitive services;
e. coverage of availability of trained prosecutors in prosecuting cases of violence against women and children; andf. coverage of availability of trained judges in handling cases of violence against women and children.

Furthermore, legal aid services can also be measured through supporting indicators, namely the coverage of availability of legal assistants or advocates who have the ability to assist witnesses and/or victims of violence against women and children.

8 - letter e Return and Social Reintegration for women and children victims of violence, with the main indicators being the coverage of return services for women and children victims of violence and the coverage of social reintegration services for women and children victims of violence.

Return and Reintegration are efforts to return victims to their area of origin to be returned to the nuclear family, substitute family, or community. Specifically for social reintegration services, it can also be measured through supporting indicators, namely the coverage of availability of trained officers to carry out social reintegration.

